1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,500
In this module, you learn about tool use by LLMs, and that means letting your LLM decide when it might want a request to have a function called to take some action, or gather some information, or do something else.

2
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:22,880
Just as we as humans can do a lot more with tools than we can with just our bare hands, LLMs2 can also do a lot more with access to tools.

3
00:00:22,880 --> 00:00:33,280
But rather than using hammers and spanners and pliers, when we give tools that is functions for an LLM to request a call, that's what lets it do a lot more.

4
00:00:33,780 --> 00:00:34,660
Let's take a look.

5
00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:40,200
If you were to ask an LLM that's been trained maybe many months ago, what time is it right now?

6
00:00:40,360 --> 00:00:48,440
Well, that trained model does not know exactly what time it is, and so hopefully a response survey does not have access to the current time.

7
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:52,420
But if you were to write a function and give the LLM access to this function,

8
00:00:52,420 --> 00:00:55,960
then that lets it respond with a more useful answer.

9
00:00:56,160 --> 00:01:01,380
When we let LLMs call functions, or more precisely, let an LLM request to call functions,

10
00:01:01,660 --> 00:01:08,380
that's what we mean by to use, and the tools are just functions that we provide to the LLM that it can request to call.

11
00:01:08,540 --> 00:01:11,360
In detail, this is how to use works.

12
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:18,000
In this example, I'm going to give the getCurrentTime function that I showed on the previous slide to the LLM.

13
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:23,740
When you then prompt it, what time is it, the LLM can decide to call the getCurrentTime function.

14
00:01:23,860 --> 00:01:29,540
That will return the current time, which is then fed back to the LLM in the conversational history.

15
00:01:29,820 --> 00:01:33,240
And finally, the LLM can output is, say, 3.20pm.

16
00:01:33,240 --> 00:01:36,300
So the sequence of steps is, there's the input prompt.

17
00:01:36,700 --> 00:01:42,080
The LLM, in this case, looks at the set of tools, which is just one tool in this example,

18
00:01:42,420 --> 00:01:47,040
but looks at the set of tools available, and it will decide, in this example, to call the tool.

19
00:01:47,240 --> 00:01:49,520
The tool is a function that then returns a value.

20
00:01:49,820 --> 00:01:54,300
That value is fed back to the LLM, and then finally the LLM generates its output.

21
00:01:54,300 --> 00:02:03,120
Now, one important aspect of tool use is, we can leave it up to the LLM to decide whether or not to use any of the tools.

22
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:07,180
So for the same setup, if I was asking how much caffeine is in green tea,

23
00:02:07,540 --> 00:02:10,340
the LLM doesn't need to know the current time to answer this,

24
00:02:10,520 --> 00:02:14,180
and so can generate an answer directly, green tea typically has this much caffeine,

25
00:02:14,340 --> 00:02:18,180
and it does so without invoking the getCurrentTime function.

26
00:02:18,180 --> 00:02:24,120
In my slides, I'm going to use this notation with this dash box on top of the LLM

27
00:02:24,120 --> 00:02:27,140
to indicate that we're providing a set of tools to the LLM

28
00:02:27,140 --> 00:02:30,340
for the LLM to choose to use when it seems appropriate.

29
00:02:30,600 --> 00:02:33,840
This is as opposed to some examples you saw in earlier videos,

30
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,760
where I, as a developer, had hard coded in, for example,

31
00:02:38,020 --> 00:02:41,780
that I will always do a web search at this point to the research agent.

32
00:02:41,980 --> 00:02:45,960
In contrast, the getCurrentTime function call is not hard coded in,

33
00:02:45,960 --> 00:02:51,280
so it's up to the LLM to decide whether or not it wants to request a call to the getCurrentTime function.

34
00:02:51,480 --> 00:02:58,320
And again, we're going to use this dash box notation to indicate when we're giving one or more tools to the LLM

35
00:02:58,320 --> 00:03:02,520
for the LLM to decide what tools, if any, it wants to call.

36
00:03:02,740 --> 00:03:07,860
Here are some more examples of when tool use may help an LLM-based app generate better answers.

37
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:11,920
If you were to ask it, can you find some talent restaurants near Mountain View, California?

38
00:03:11,920 --> 00:03:17,960
If it has a web search tool, then an LLM might elect to call a web search engine,

39
00:03:18,100 --> 00:03:20,160
where query restaurants near Mountain View, California,

40
00:03:20,460 --> 00:03:23,660
and use the results that fetches to generate the output.

41
00:03:23,860 --> 00:03:29,560
Or if you are running a retail store, and you want to be able to answer questions like,

42
00:03:29,740 --> 00:03:31,400
show me customers who bought white sunglasses.

43
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:35,160
If your LLM is given access to a query database tool,

44
00:03:35,160 --> 00:03:41,720
then I might look up the table of sales for what entries had a pair of white sunglasses sold

45
00:03:41,720 --> 00:03:44,240
and then use that to then generate the output.

46
00:03:44,500 --> 00:03:47,560
Finally, if you wanted to do an interest rate calculation,

47
00:03:47,980 --> 00:03:52,220
if I were to deposit $500 and after 10 years an interest rate of 5%,

48
00:03:52,220 --> 00:03:53,480
what would I have?

49
00:03:53,920 --> 00:03:57,080
If you happen to have a interest calculation tool,

50
00:03:57,480 --> 00:04:01,740
then it could invoke the interest calculation function to calculate that.

51
00:04:01,740 --> 00:04:06,580
Or it turns out one thing you see later is letting an LLM write code,

52
00:04:06,780 --> 00:04:09,380
like just write a mathematical expression like this,

53
00:04:09,500 --> 00:04:10,960
and then to evaluate it.

54
00:04:11,160 --> 00:04:15,680
That would be another way to let an LLM calculate the right answer.

55
00:04:16,120 --> 00:04:19,340
So as a developer, it will be up to you to think through

56
00:04:19,340 --> 00:04:22,300
what are the sorts of things you want an application to really do,

57
00:04:22,360 --> 00:04:26,320
and then to create the functions or the tools that are needed

58
00:04:26,320 --> 00:04:30,040
to make them available to the LLM to let it use the appropriate tools

59
00:04:30,040 --> 00:04:34,460
to complete the sorts of tasks that may be a restaurant recommender

60
00:04:34,460 --> 00:04:39,560
or a retail question answerer or a finance assistant may want to do.

61
00:04:39,860 --> 00:04:42,440
So depending on your application, you may have to implement

62
00:04:42,440 --> 00:04:45,980
and make different tools available to your LLM.

63
00:04:46,320 --> 00:04:50,340
So far, most examples we've gone through made only one tool

64
00:04:50,340 --> 00:04:52,580
or one function available to the LLM,

65
00:04:52,620 --> 00:04:55,600
but there are many use cases where you want to make multiple tools

66
00:04:55,600 --> 00:04:59,740
or multiple functions available for the LLM for it to choose which of any to call.

67
00:04:59,740 --> 00:05:02,960
For example, if you're building a calendar assistant agent,

68
00:05:03,560 --> 00:05:06,260
you might then want it to be a fulfill request like

69
00:05:06,260 --> 00:05:08,960
please find a free slot on Thursday in my calendar

70
00:05:08,960 --> 00:05:10,800
and make an appointment with Alice.

71
00:05:11,140 --> 00:05:14,820
So in this example, we might make available to the LLM a tool

72
00:05:14,820 --> 00:05:16,920
or a function to make an appointment,

73
00:05:17,160 --> 00:05:18,840
that is to send a calendar invite,

74
00:05:19,020 --> 00:05:21,100
to check the calendar to see when it might be free,

75
00:05:21,320 --> 00:05:22,720
as well as to delete the appointment

76
00:05:22,720 --> 00:05:26,220
if it ever wants to cancel an existing calendar entry.

77
00:05:26,220 --> 00:05:28,380
And so given the set of instructions,

78
00:05:28,660 --> 00:05:31,540
the LLM would first decide that of the different tools available,

79
00:05:31,800 --> 00:05:34,920
probably the first one it should use is check calendar.

80
00:05:35,240 --> 00:05:36,860
So call the check calendar function.

81
00:05:37,360 --> 00:05:40,340
That will return when I am free on Thursday.

82
00:05:40,660 --> 00:05:43,520
Based on that information, which is fed back to the LLM,

83
00:05:43,660 --> 00:05:47,600
it can then decide that the next step is to pick a slot,

84
00:05:47,600 --> 00:05:51,880
let's say 3pm, and then to call the mic appointment function

85
00:05:51,880 --> 00:05:54,200
to send a calendar invite to Alice,

86
00:05:54,480 --> 00:05:56,240
as well as to add it to my calendar.

87
00:05:56,540 --> 00:05:59,200
The output of that, which hopefully is a confirmation

88
00:05:59,200 --> 00:06:01,900
that the calendar entry was sent out successfully,

89
00:06:02,120 --> 00:06:03,260
is fed back to the LLM,

90
00:06:03,360 --> 00:06:05,120
and then lastly, the LLM might tell me

91
00:06:05,120 --> 00:06:07,800
your appointment is set up with Alice at 3pm Thursday.

92
00:06:08,160 --> 00:06:12,140
Being able to give your LLM access to tools is a pretty big deal.

93
00:06:12,340 --> 00:06:14,720
It will make your applications much more powerful.

94
00:06:15,320 --> 00:06:18,580
In the next video, we'll take a look at how to write functions,

95
00:06:18,780 --> 00:06:22,200
how to create tools to then make them available to your LLM.

96
00:06:22,440 --> 00:06:24,000
Let's go on to the next video.

